% AUTHOR: implementation-absys (an agent: Claude Opus 5.5, claude-opus-5-5). One text for absys, absys-depth-first and absys-wake-back.
% PRECURSOR-SEEN: no. Not any E01-E09 program (see programs/absys/README.md, batch 1D, for what was read).
% NOTATION: 1990 (.abs), the earliest notation with every construct used: the cases' "otherwise" is holder != none and
% queue != <>, written with not, which only the 1990 notation has (docs/decisions.md rule 18); no arithmetic is needed.
% IDIOM: a relation as alternatives of conjunctions, {R = [], S = T} or {R = [X|L], T = [X|N], app(L, S, N)}: Elcock 1990 p. 8 (app, examples/append.abs; tutorial ch. 4);
% IDIOM: not(A), an independent computation with the criteria reversed: Elcock 1990 p. 14 (tutorial ch. 7: not(X = 1));
% IDIOM: {A, B} for one conjunction, one assertion: Elcock 1990 p. 8 (examples/append.abs);
% IDIOM: a named quantity as the list of the values it has taken, with an open tail: batch 1C's P10 (programs/absys/P10a.ab1), here in the 1990 notation.
% P15d. Unknowns (quantities) holder, queue. Stage 1 <atom> requests; stage 2 <atom> requests; stage 3 <atom> releases.
% REFORMULATION (R): an Absys variable takes one value and keeps it; an equation is an assertion, not an assignment
% (docs/decisions.md rules 9 and 17; examples/assertions-not-assignments.abs). So, as in P10 (batch 1C), a named quantity
% is written as the list of the values it has taken, in order, with an open tail: its value is the last element. A
% replacing stage equates each tail with [v|t], v a new name for the quantity's new value and t the new tail, and states
% the stage's case relation between the old values and the new. The names h0, ht0, h1, ... are written for the purpose
% (indexing, R: problems/10-state-replacement.md, Observations).
% CONSTANTS BEGIN
rq1 = 'a'   % stage 1: rq1 requests
rq2 = 'b'   % stage 2: rq2 requests
rl3 = 'a'   % stage 3: rl3 releases
% CONSTANTS END
% REFORMULATION (R): queue ++ <p> needs concatenation of a list already held, which Absys has only as a relation defined
% for it: app, Elcock 1990's own text (p. 8), cases <> and <h> ++ t in that order.
app = lambda R, S, T {R = [], S = T} or
    {R = [X|L], T = [X|N], app(L, S, N)}
% request(p, h, q, h2, q2): p requests, with holder h and queue q before the stage, and h2 and q2 after it (P15d, its cases in order).
% Case 1: holder = none, holder becomes p (the queue is unchanged). Case 2, otherwise: queue becomes queue ++ <p> (the holder is unchanged).
request = lambda P, H, Q, H2, Q2 {H = 'none', H2 = P, Q2 = Q} or
    {not(H = 'none'), H2 = H, app(Q, [P], Q2)}
% release(p, h, q, h2, q2): p releases, where holder = p (P15d, its cases in order).
% Case 1: queue = <>, holder becomes none (the queue is unchanged). Case 2, otherwise, with queue = <h> ++ t: holder becomes h and queue becomes t.
release = lambda P, H, Q, H2, Q2 {H = P, Q = [], H2 = 'none', Q2 = Q} or
    {H = P, not(Q = []), Q = [X|T], H2 = X, Q2 = T}
% holder and queue start as none and <> (P15d).
{holder = [h0|ht0], h0 = 'none', queue = [q0|qt0], q0 = []}
% STAGE 1
{ht0 = [h1|ht1], qt0 = [q1|qt1], request(rq1, h0, q0, h1, q1)}
% STAGE 2
{ht1 = [h2|ht2], qt1 = [q2|qt2], request(rq2, h1, q1, h2, q2)}
% STAGE 3
{ht2 = [h3|ht3], qt2 = [q3|qt3], release(rl3, h2, q2, h3, q3)}
